/** YD001 mechaber quality-pass siman 331 (merged) */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  '1#main':
    'The law of giving them in which place and how the kohen receives them, and whether they are d\'oraisa or d\'rabbanan. And it contains 146 seifim: Terumot and ma\'asrot are not practiced d\'oraisa except in Eretz Yisrael, whether when the Temple stands or when it does not stand. And the prophets enacted that they apply even in the land of Shinar, because it is adjacent to Eretz Yisrael and most of Israel go and return there. And the early sages enacted that they apply also in the land of Egypt and the land of Ammon and Moav, because they surround Eretz Yisrael. Syria—in which one who acquires a field is obligated in terumot and ma\'asrot—it is from the words of the scribes. And the rest of the lands, terumot and ma\'asrot do not apply in them:',
  '2#main':
    'At this time, even in a place that the exiles of Babylonia held, and even in the days of Ezra, there is no obligation of terumot and ma\'asrot d\'oraisa but from their words, because it is said "when you come"—it implies the coming of all of you, and not the coming of part of them, as it was in the days of Ezra: {Rama: And there are those who disagree, and their reasoning is correct that they are now obligated in Eretz Yisrael in terumot and ma\'asrot d\'oraisa, but they did not practice so (Tur in the name of R. Yitzchak).}',
  '3#main':
    'A non-Jew who bought land in Eretz Yisrael and Israel returned and took it from him—he separates terumot and ma\'asrot; which is not so in Syria:',
  '4#main':
    'The fruits of a non-Jew that grew on land he bought in Eretz Yisrael—if their work was completed in the hand of the non-Jew and he trod them, they are exempt from all of them. And if an Israelite took them after they were detached before their work was completed and he completed their work, they are obligated. And he separates terumah and ma\'aser rishon and terumat ma\'aser, and leaves for himself ma\'aser rishon and it is his, because he says to the Levi, "I come by virtue of a man" that you cannot take from him. (And terumat ma\'aser he sells to the kohen and takes its value) (Tur):',
  '5#main':
    'A non-Jew sold his fruits to an Israelite while they were attached to the ground—if before they reached the season of ma\'asrot and they were completed in the hand of the Israelite, he is obligated in all of them and gives all ma\'aser rishon to the Levi. And if he sold them after they reached the season of ma\'asrot, he separates terumot and ma\'asrot and gives the ma\'aser to the Levi according to the calculation. How so? He took grain that was sown from a non-Jew after it brought a third and it was completed in the hand of the Israelite—he separates terumot and ma\'asrot and gives to the Levi two-thirds of ma\'aser rishon:',
  '6#main':
    'An Israelite who sold his fruits to a non-Jew before they reached the season of ma\'asrot and the non-Jew completed them—they are exempt from terumah and from ma\'asrot. And if after they reached the season of ma\'asrot—even though the non-Jew completed them, he is obligated in all of them:',
  '7#main':
    'A non-Jew sold to an Israelite fruits attached after they reached the season of ma\'asrot and the non-Jew trod them in the domain of the Israelite—they are not obligated in terumah and ma\'asrot, since they reached the season of ma\'asrot in the domain of the non-Jew and he trod them, even though they are in the domain of the Israelite. One who buys fruits from a non-Jew in Syria, whether detached or attached, even before they reached the season of ma\'asrot—even though an Israelite trod them, they are exempt. And if they have not yet reached the season of ma\'asrot, since he acquired them with the land, he is obligated to tithe:',
  '8#main':
    'An Israelite who was a sharecropper for a non-Jew in Syria—his fruits are exempt, because he has nothing in the body of the land itself. And likewise the contractor, the recipient, and the renter of a field from a non-Jew in Syria—their fruits are exempt:',
  '9#main':
    'An Israelite who took a field in Syria from a non-Jew before it brought a third and returned and sold it to another non-Jew after it brought a third—if Israel returned and took it a second time, he is obligated, for behold he became obligated in the hand of the Israelite:',
  '10#main':
    'An Israelite who had land in Syria and brought down a sharecropper to it and the sharecropper sent him the fruits—behold these are exempt, for I say he took them from the market; and this is when that species is available in the market:',
  '11#main':
    'A partnership with a non-Jew is obligated in terumah and ma\'asrot. How so? An Israelite and a non-Jew who took a field in partnership—even if they divided the field while standing, and needless to say if they divided the stack—behold this is tevel and chullin mixed in every stalk and stalk of the portion of the non-Jew, even though the non-Jew trod them, and they are obligated from their words. In what case? In Eretz Yisrael, where the ma\'asrot are of Torah, and in that of Torah there is no clarification; but if they took a field in Syria, since the ma\'asrot there are from their words—even if they divided the stack, the portion of the non-Jew is exempt from everything. And it appears to me that nowadays, when there is no obligation of terumot and ma\'asrot in Eretz Yisrael except from their words, also in Eretz Yisrael the portion of the non-Jew is exempt from everything:',
  '12#main':
    'Fruits of Eretz Yisrael that left outside the land are exempt from terumot and ma\'asrot. And if they went to Syria, they are obligated. And fruits outside the land that entered the land are obligated if they were fixed for ma\'aser [in the hand of an Israelite] after they entered the land:',
  '13#main':
    'Every human food whose growth is from the land is obligated in terumah:',
  '14#main':
    'One does not separate terumot and ma\'asrot from vegetables outside the land even in places where we said the sages obligated them. And likewise vegetables that come from outside the land to the land—even though they have earth at their roots, behold this is exempt:',
  '15#main':
    'Grain and olives that did not bring a third are exempt from terumah and ma\'asrot. And from where does one know? Everything that is sown and grows—it is known that it brought a third:',
  '16#main':
    'Hefker is exempt from terumah and ma\'asrot. But one who sows a hefker field is obligated in terumah and ma\'asrot:',
  '17#main':
    'Things that most people do not sow in gardens and fields but their presumption is from hefker are exempt from terumah and ma\'asrot:',
  '18#main':
    'Fruits obligated in terumah and ma\'aser that became mixed with exempt fruits—if he has other obligated fruits, he takes out from them according to the calculation of the obligated fruits in the mixture. And if not, he takes out terumah and terumat ma\'aser on everything and separates ma\'aser rishon and sheni according to the calculation of the obligated fruits in the mixture:',
  '19#main':
    'Terumah gedolah—at this time, when it stands to be burned because of tumah—its measure is whatever amount it is: {Rama: And one gives it at this time to any kohen he wishes, whether a chaver or an am ha\'aretz, and even if he is not of distinguished lineage, only that he is presumed to be a kohen and he burns it. And he may leave it and burn it [until] he can benefit from it at the time of burning; but a zar is forbidden to benefit from it at the time of its burning unless a kohen benefits with him. But other benefits that do not consume it are permitted even to zarim (Tur). A kohen who eats at an Israelite\'s—he gives him with terumah oil for a lamp, and even though the kohen stands and goes, one need not extinguish it until it goes out by itself (Mordechai chapter HaAlmanah for 23). And all this speaks of terumah that was made fit, for then it became tamei and is permitted to be burned; but if it was not made fit and did not become tamei, it is forbidden to burn it, and it is good to make it fit before treading so that it receives tumah and will be permitted to be burned (Tur).} And afterward he separates from the remainder one in ten, and it is called ma\'aser rishon, and gives it to the Levi (and some say even to the kohen) (Tur). And on this it is said in the Torah, "For the tithe of the children of Israel," etc., "and to the children of Levi, behold I have given all the tithe in Israel," etc. And afterward he separates from the remainder one in ten, and it is called ma\'aser sheni, and it belongs to its owner; and in this siman its laws will be explained. And on it it is said, "You shall surely tithe." And in this order one separates in the first year of the shemittah cycle and in the second and fourth and fifth. But in the third and sixth of the shemittah cycle, after separating ma\'aser rishon he separates from the remainder one in ten and gives it to the poor, and it is called ma\'aser ani. And in these two years there is no ma\'aser sheni but ma\'aser ani. And on it it is said, "At the end of three years you shall bring forth all the tithe of your produce," etc., "and the Levite shall come," etc. The shemittah year—all of it is hefker and has in it neither terumah nor ma\'asrot at all. And outside the land, where there is no shemittah of land, one separates in the shemittah year in the land of Egypt and in Ammon and Moav ma\'aser rishon and ma\'aser ani. And in the land of Shinar one separates ma\'aser sheni as in most years. And the true shemittah year was the year 5333. (And some say if a non-Jew sold fruits in the shevi\'it to an Israelite and he completed their work in the hand of the Israelite, they are obligated in terumah and ma\'aser and he separates ma\'aser ani as the law of Ammon and Moav) (Beit Yosef, and so wrote Kaptor VaPerach):',
  '20#main':
    'Ma\'aser rishon that the Levi takes—he separates from it one in ten and gives it to the kohen, and it is called terumat ma\'aser. And on it it is said, "And to the Levites you shall speak," etc.: (And the law of terumat ma\'aser is like the law of terumah gedolah; however, even nowadays we require giving the full measure) (Beit Yosef in the name of the Rambam):',
  '21#main':
    'One who increases in terumah—since he left part chullin, behold this is terumah. But if he said, "All these fruits are terumah," he has said nothing:',
  '22#main':
    'One who says, "The terumah of this stack is inside it"—if he said in its north or in its south, he has named a place and is obligated to separate his terumah from it. And if he did not specify the place, he has said nothing:',
  '23#main':
    'He said, "The terumah of this stack is in that one, and the terumah of that stack is in this one"—in the place where the terumah of the first one was completed, there the terumah of the second was completed:',
  '24#main':
    'Terumat ma\'aser—one does not separate it by estimate but is precise with its measure, and even at this time. A thing that is customarily measured—he measures; a thing that is weighed—he weighs; a thing that can be counted—he counts. If it was possible to count it and weigh it and measure it—the counter is praiseworthy, and the measurer is more praiseworthy than he, and the weigher is more praiseworthy than both of them: (And it is taken not from the surrounding area; and Torah scholars separate terumah only from the surrounding area) (Rambam chapter 3):',
  '25#main':
    'One does not separate terumah gedolah except from the surrounding area. How so? He had fifty se\'ah in this house and fifty se\'ah in another house—he may not separate from one of them for both of them. And if he separated not from the surrounding area, his terumah is terumah, provided that the separated portion is guarded. But if he was laden with a load of wine or oil and saw that they were breaking and said, "Behold these are terumah for the fruits in my house," he has said nothing. And it appears to me that at this time, when it goes to destruction because of tumah, his terumah is terumah:',
  '26#main':
    'Fruits scattered inside the house, or two granaries (meaning: places where fruits are brought in, from the matter of "his granary in his domain") in one house—he separates terumah from one for all of them. (A stack of grain and round cakes of figs and barrels of dried figs—if they were in one enclosure, he separates from one for all of them.) Barrels [that should read: of wine, and so in the Rambam chapter 3 law 18 and as stated in the Yerushalmi in tractate Ma\'aserot that specifically of wine and not of oil, see there (Mar\'eh Kohen)] whose mouths were not sealed—he separates from one for all of them; once sealed, he separates from each and every one:',
  '27#main':
    'One who precedes ma\'aser sheni to rishon, or ma\'aser rishon to terumah—even though he transgressed a negative commandment, what he did is done:',
  '28#main':
    'One who wishes to separate terumah and terumat ma\'aser at once—he separates one in a hundred and a little more, and says: "One in a hundred that is here—behold it is on this side that I separated, and behold it is chullin; and the remainder of what I separated, which is the slight excess over one in a hundred, is terumah on everything. And the ma\'aser that ought to be for this hundred chullin—behold it is on this side that I separated; and this remainder more than the terumah from what I separated—behold it is terumat ma\'aser on everything:',
  '29#main':
    'A person may appoint an agent to separate terumot and ma\'asrot:',
  '30#main':
    'Five [types of people]—if they separated terumah, their terumah is not terumah: a deaf-mute, an imbecile, a minor, and a non-Jew who separated terumah of an Israelite even with his permission, and one who separates what is not his without permission of the owners. But one who separates his own for others—behold this is terumah, and he fixes their fruits; and the benefit of his favor he may give to any kohen he wishes:',
  '31#main':
    'One who separates terumah without permission, or who went down into another\'s field and gathered fruits without permission in order to take them and separate terumah—if the owner of the house came and said to him, "Take for yourself the fine ones"—if there were fine ones there beyond what he separated, his terumah is terumah; and if not, his terumah is not terumah. And if the owner of the house gathered and added—whether in this case or that case, his terumah is terumah:',
  '32#main':
    'One who speaks and does not hear, or hears and does not speak, and the cunning, the drunkard, and the blind—let them not separate terumah; and if they separated, their terumah is terumah. And at this time, when it goes to burning because of tumah, one separates terumah with a drunkard and a blind person even l\'chatchila; but not ma\'aser for a Levi:',
  '33#main':
    'A minor who reached the age of vows—even though he did not bring two hairs and did not become an adult—if he separated terumah, his terumah is terumah:',
  '34#main':
    'One who says to his agent, "Go and separate terumah for me," and he went to separate terumah and does not know whether he separated, and he came and found a stack—its presumption is not terumah; we are concerned perhaps another separated without permission: {Rama: And some say that in such a case its presumption is terumah, for we say the presumption is that an agent performs his agency even in a d\'oraisa prohibition; but if he does not know whether the stack is terumah or not, it is forbidden to eat from it, for the agent says he certainly did not touch it until he knows it is terumah (Tur in the name of the Rosh and R. Shimon).}',
  '35#main':
    'Fruits of partners are obligated in terumah and ma\'asrot, and they need not take permission from each other; rather, whoever separates terumah among them—his terumah is terumah. One of them separated and his fellow did not know and he came and separated—if they protest against each other, the terumah of the second is not terumah; and if not, if the first separated the full measure, the terumah of the second is not terumah; and if the first did not separate the full measure, the terumah of both of them is terumah. And it appears to me that at this time this is so for ma\'aser; but for terumah, since terumah has no measure, whether in this way or that way the terumah of the second is not terumah:',
  '36#main':
    'One who says to one, "Go separate terumah," and he went to separate terumah and he canceled his agency before he separated terumah—if the agent changed his agency, such as that he said to him "separate from the north" and he separated from the south, his terumah is not terumah; and if not, his terumah is terumah. (And there are those who disagree and hold that whenever agency was canceled, even if he did not change, his terumah is not terumah.) (Tur):',
  '37#main':
    'A sharecropper who separated terumah and the owner of the house came and stopped him—if he stopped him before he separated terumah, his terumah is not terumah; and if he stopped him after he separated terumah, his terumah is terumah:',
  '38#main':
    'Guardians separate the property of orphans:',
  '39#main':
    'A thief, robber, and one who takes by force—their terumah is terumah. And if the owners were pursuing after them, their terumah is not terumah:',
  '40#main':
    'A son, hired worker, slave, and woman separate terumah on what they eat, but not on the rest:',
  '41#main':
    'A son when he eats with his father, and a woman in her dough—separate terumah, because they are in his domain:',
  '42#main':
    'Workers do not have permission to separate terumah without the knowledge of the owner of the house:',
  '43#main':
    'A worker to whom the owner of the house said, "Gather my grain for me and separate terumah," and he separated terumah and afterward gathered—his terumah is terumah:',
  '44#main':
    'A non-Jew who separated terumah from his own—the sages decreed that his terumah is terumah if he is in Eretz Yisrael: {Rama: And they examine him—if he said with the intent of Israel, "I separated," it is given to the kohen; and if not, it requires burial (Tur).}',
  '45#main':
    'One who intended to say terumah and said ma\'aser, or ma\'aser and said terumah—he has said nothing until his mouth and heart are aligned:',
  '46#main':
    'One who separated terumah in his thought and did not express it with his lips—behold this is terumah, as it is said, "And it shall be reckoned to you as your terumah:',
  '47#main':
    'One who separates terumah conditionally—if the condition is fulfilled, behold this is terumah:',
  '48#main':
    'One who separated terumot and ma\'asrot and regretted them—behold he asks a sage and he permits him as they permit other vows, and they return to be chullin as they were:',
  '49#main':
    'One who says, "What is above is terumah and what is below is chullin," or the reverse—his words stand:',
  '50#main':
    'One who separates terumah from the threshing floor must intend in his heart that the terumah be for the stack and for what is in the cut stalks (meaning: small stalks that were not threshed) and for what is at the sides and for what is inside the straw. One who separates terumah from the winepress must intend in his heart to separate terumah for what is in the husks and for what is in the lees. One who separates terumah from the pit of oil must intend in his heart for what is in the refuse (meaning: waste of olives). And if he did not intend but separated unconditionally, everything is discharged, for it is a condition of the court that terumah is on everything:',
  '51#main':
    'One who separates terumah from a basket of figs and figs are found at the side of the basket—behold these are exempt, because in his heart he intended to separate terumah on everything:',
  '52#main':
    'One separates terumah only from the best. And it appears to me that nowadays, when it goes to destruction because of tumah, one need not be strict about this; however, in ma\'aser that is given to the Levi and to the poor, one should be strict about this:',
  '53#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from one species for what is not its species; and if he separated, his terumah is not terumah. Gourds and cucumbers are one species; every species of wheat is one species; every species of figs, dried figs, and fig cakes is one species, and he separates from one for the other. And everything that is kilayim with its fellow—he may not separate terumah from this for that, even from the good for the bad; and if he separated, his terumah is not terumah. And everything that is not kilayim with its fellow—he separates from the good for the bad, but not from the bad for the good; and if he separated, his terumah is terumah—except for zunin (meaning: black seeds found among wheat) for wheat, because people do not eat them:',
  '54#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from a thing whose work was completed for a thing whose work was not completed, nor from a thing whose work was not completed for a thing whose work was completed; and if he separated, his terumah is terumah:',
  '55#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from what is attached for what is detached, nor from what is detached for what is attached; and if he separated, his terumah is not terumah. But if he said, "The fruits of this garden bed—when they are detached they shall be terumah for the fruits of this garden bed when they are detached," and they were detached—his words stand; and this is when both brought a third at the time he spoke:',
  '56#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from the moist for the dry, nor from the dry for the moist; and if he separated, his terumah is terumah:',
  '57#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from the fruits of this year for the fruits of last year, nor from the fruits of last year for the fruits of this year; and if he separated, it is not terumah, as it is said, "year by year." One who gathered vegetables on the eve of Rosh Hashanah before the sun set and returned and gathered after the sun set—he does not separate terumah from this for that, for this is new and that is old. And likewise if he took an etrog on the eve of the 15th of Shevat before the sun set and returned and took an etrog after the sun set—he does not separate terumah from this for that, because the 1st of Tishrei is Rosh Hashanah for ma\'asrot of grain, legumes, and vegetables, and the 15th of Shevat is Rosh Hashanah for ma\'asrot of trees:',
  '58#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from fruits of the land for fruits outside the land, nor from fruits outside the land for fruits of the land, nor from exempt fruits for obligated fruits, nor from obligated fruits for exempt fruits; and if they separated, their terumah is not terumah:',
  '59#main':
    'One who separated terumah from demai (meaning: fruit or grain taken from amei ha\'aretz, where it is not known whether they separated ma\'aser properly, and it is obligated in ma\'asrot d\'rabbanan) for demai or for vadai—his terumah is terumah, and he returns and separates terumah from each one by itself:',
  '60#main':
    'One who separated terumah from vadai for demai—his terumah is terumah, and he may not eat until he takes out terumah and ma\'asrot on it:',
  '61#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from what was bought for what grew inside his field, nor what was bought from a non-Jew for what was bought from an Israelite, or the reverse:',
  '62#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from stalks for grain, nor from olives for oil, nor from grapes for wine; but one separates oil for olives that are pickled, and wine for grapes to make dried grapes:',
  '63#main':
    'One separates oil olives for pickled olives, but not from pickled olives for oil olives; from wine that is not cooked for what is cooked, but not from what is cooked for what is not cooked; from clear for what is not clear, but not from what is not clear for clear; from figs for dried figs by count, from dried figs for figs by measure—but not figs for dried figs by measure, nor dried figs for figs by count, so that one always separate with a favorable eye. And one separates wheat for bread, but not from bread for wheat, according to the calculation. And in all these, if he separated, his terumah is terumah. And it appears to me that nowadays, when terumah goes to destruction, one need not be strict about this; and likewise in other similar matters. However, in ma\'aser that is given to the Levi and to the poor, one should be strict about this:',
  '64#main':
    'One does not separate terumah from vinegar for wine, but one separates wine for vinegar:',
  '65#main':
    'One who separates terumah from a barrel of wine for wine and it is found to be vinegar—if it is known that it was vinegar before he separated terumah, it is not terumah; and if it soured after he separated terumah, behold this is terumah. If in doubt—it is terumah, and he returns and separates terumah. And likewise one who separates a gourd and it is found bitter, a watermelon and it is found rotten:',
  '66#main':
    'After one separates terumah gedolah, he separates one in ten from what remains, and it is called ma\'aser rishon:',
  '67#main':
    'Ma\'aser rishon is permitted to be eaten by an Israelite, and it is permitted to eat it in tumah. Therefore a daughter of a Levi who was defiled by licentious intercourse—they give her ma\'aser and she eats. But one who heard that her husband died, or one witness testified to her and she remarried, and afterward her husband came—the sages penalized her that she be forbidden in ma\'aser:',
  '68#main':
    'Levites and kohanim separate ma\'aser rishon in order to separate terumat ma\'aser from it; and likewise kohanim separate the rest of terumot and ma\'asrot, and they are for themselves:',
  '69#main':
    'One does not take ma\'aser from the hand of kohanim; and likewise all gifts of kehunah—one does not take them from kohen to kohen:',
  '70#main':
    'One who eats his fruits tevel, and likewise a Levi who ate ma\'aser while tevel—even though they are liable to death at the hands of Heaven, they do not pay the gifts to their owners, as it is said, "that they raise to the Lord"—you have nothing in them until they raise them. And outside the land it is permitted for a person to eat and go first and afterward separate terumah and ma\'asrot:',
  '71#main':
    'One tithes from this place for another place, and he need not tithe from the surrounding area; but one does not tithe from one species for what is not its species, nor from obligated for exempt, nor from exempt for obligated; and if he tithed, it is not ma\'aser:',
  '72#main':
    'Everything we said regarding terumah—that one does not separate terumah from this for that—so too regarding ma\'aser one does not tithe from this for that. And everything we said regarding terumah, if he separated terumah his terumah is terumah—so too regarding ma\'aser, if he separated his ma\'asrot they are ma\'asrot. And everything exempt from terumah is exempt from ma\'aser; and everything that separates ma\'aser:',
  '73#main':
    'Everything we said about them—that they may not separate terumah, and if they separated their terumah is terumah—so too if they tithed, their ma\'asrot are ma\'asrot. And everything whose terumah is not terumah—so too if he took out ma\'aser, it is not ma\'aser:',
  '74#main':
    'Bitter almonds, whether in their growth or when small, are exempt, because they are not eaten:',
  '75#main':
    'One tithes only from the best:',
  '76#main':
    'One does not tithe by estimate but by measure, or by weight, or by count; and whoever is precise with the measure is praiseworthy. And one who increases in ma\'asrot—his ma\'asrot are defective, for behold tevel is mixed in them, and his fruits are fixed:',
  '77#main':
    'One who separates part of ma\'aser—it is not ma\'aser, but like one who divided the pile; but he must take out his ma\'aser from this portion. How so? He had a hundred se\'ah and separated five of them for ma\'aser—it is not ma\'aser, and he cannot separate ma\'aser on the five se\'ah from another place, but from them half a se\'ah, which is their ma\'aser:',
  '78#main':
    'One who separates terumah gedolah or terumat ma\'aser blesses before separating: "...Who sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us to separate terumah." And likewise one who separates ma\'aser rishon blesses before separating ma\'aser; and likewise he blesses on ma\'aser sheni. And if he separated everything one after another immediately and did not speak between them, he includes them in one blessing and blesses "...to separate terumot and ma\'asrot:',
  '79#main':
    'Every fruit that from its smallness is fit to be eaten and one does not leave it except to add to its body—immediately it is obligated in ma\'asrot. And every fruit that is not fit to be eaten when small is not obligated in ma\'asrot until it reaches the season of ma\'asrot:',
  '80#main':
    'A cluster in which even a single berry reached—the whole cluster is connected for ma\'asrot, as if the whole reached, and not that cluster alone but all the wind that is in that vine in which the cluster is. And likewise a pomegranate in which even one section reached—the whole is connected:',
  '81#main':
    'Wine lees to which one gave water and strained them—if he gave three parts water and found four parts liquid, he takes out ma\'aser from this mixture from another place, and he does not separate terumah on it, for the one who separates terumah at the outset intends in his heart on everything. And if he found less than four, even though he found more than his measure, and even though they have the taste of wine—they are exempt:',
  '82#main':
    'Fruits that reached the season of ma\'asrot and were detached and their work is not yet completed—such as grain that was reaped and threshed but not yet winnowed or trodden—it is permitted to eat from them a casual eating until their work is completed. And once their work is completed, it is forbidden to eat from them casually—in what case? When he completes his fruits to sell them in the market; but if his intent was to bring them to the house, behold this is permitted to eat from them casually after their work was completed until they are fixed for ma\'aser:',
  '83#main':
    'Four things fix fruits for ma\'asrot: the house, sale, fire, and salt; terumah; and Shabbat. And all of them fix only a thing whose work was completed. How so? Fruits that he intends to bring to the house—even though their work was completed, he eats from them casual eating until they enter his house. They entered his house—they are fixed for ma\'asrot and it is forbidden to eat from them until he tithes. And just as the house fixes for ma\'aser, so the courtyard fixes for ma\'aser—if the fruits entered the courtyard through the gate, they are fixed even though they did not enter inside the house. And likewise if he sold them, or cooked them in fire, or pickled them in salt, or separated terumah from them, or Shabbat entered upon them—he may not eat until he tithes, even though they did not reach the house. He brought them into the house before their work was completed—he eats from them casually. He began [to complete] their work after they entered the house—he is obligated to tithe everything. How so? He brought in gourds and melons before he rubbed them—once he begins to rub them, they are all fixed for ma\'aser; and likewise everything similar. And likewise one who separates terumah from fruits whose work was not completed—it is permitted to eat from them casually, except for a basket of figs—for if he separated terumah before its work was completed, it is fixed for ma\'aser:',
  '84#main':
    'It is permitted to employ a stratagem with grain to bring it in chaff so that his animal eats and is exempt from ma\'aser, and he winnows little by little after he brought it into the house, and it is forever exempt from terumah and ma\'asrot, for behold he does not begin to complete everything:',
  '85#main':
    'One who completes his fellow\'s fruits without his knowledge—behold these are tevel for ma\'aser:',
  '86#main':
    'What is completion of work? Wine—when he puts it in a barrel and the dregs and lees settle above the mouth of the barrel; but when it is inside the pit, when he raises it to set it in a barrel, he drinks casually and draws from the upper winepress and from the pipe and from the refuse, and drinks:',
  '87#main':
    'What is casual eating? Such as one who was peeling barley and eats from what he peels one by one; and if he peeled and put into his hand, he is obligated to tithe. He was threshing handfuls of wheat, winnowing from hand to hand and eating; and if he winnowed into his lap, he is obligated to tithe. And likewise one who takes from wine and puts into a cold dish and eats—but not into a pot, even though it is cold, because it is like a small pit. And likewise one who squeezes olives on his flesh—but not into his hand; and likewise everything similar:',
  '88#main':
    'Just as it is permitted to eat casually from fruits whose work was not completed, so it is permitted to feed them to a beast, animal, and birds as much as he wishes, and to declare them hefker as much as he wishes before he tithes. And if he completed them, even though they were not fixed for ma\'aser, he may not declare them hefker nor feed a beast, animal, or birds a fixed meal until he tithes. And it is permitted to feed a beast casually from tevel, even inside the house, and he feeds it from sheaves (meaning: bundles of grain placed in straw) of a stack until they are made into bundles:',
  '89#main':
    'A fig tree standing in a courtyard—he eats from it one by one and is exempt; and if he combined them, he is obligated in ma\'aser. In what case? When he was standing on the ground; but if he went up to the top of the fig tree, filled his lap and eats there—for the air of the courtyard does not fix for ma\'aser:',
  '90#main':
    'It was standing in the courtyard and leaned into the garden—he eats from it in the garden in his usual way, as if it were planted in the garden. It was planted in the garden and leaned into the courtyard—behold this is like planted in the courtyard, that he does not eat there except one by one:',
  '91#main':
    'A vine planted in a courtyard—he should not take the whole cluster at once and eat, but berry by berry. And likewise a pomegranate—he should not take the whole pomegranate but picks the pomegranate on the tree and eats the sections from there. And likewise a watermelon—he cuts its end (meaning: he cuts it) in the ground and eats there:',
  '92#main':
    'He was eating from a cluster in the garden and entered from the garden to the courtyard—even though he left the courtyard, he may not finish until he tithes:',
  '93#main':
    'Grain sown in the courtyard—he nibbles leaf by leaf and eats it; and if he combined them, he is obligated to tithe. And likewise everything similar:',
  '94#main':
    'One who buys detached fruits to eat them—they are fixed for ma\'aser as explained. And from when are they fixed? From when he gives money, even though he did not pull:',
  '95#main':
    'If he did not give money and was selecting and placing, selecting and placing—even the whole day—and even though he decided in his heart to buy, he is not obligated to tithe. And if he was God-fearing, from when he decided in his heart he tithes, and afterward returns to the seller if he wishes to return:',
  '96#main':
    'One who buys attached to the ground, or who bought detached to send to his fellow—they are not fixed, and he may eat from them casually:',
  '97#main':
    'One who says to his fellow, "Here is an issar for you and give me five figs for it"—he eats one by one and is exempt; and if he combined them, he is obligated to tithe. "Here is an issar for you for twenty figs that I will select for myself"—he selects one by one and eats from a cluster that he selects for himself berry by berry on the tree and eats; from a pomegranate that he selects for himself he picks it on the tree and eats; from a watermelon that he selects for himself he cuts its end in the ground and eats. And if he cut the figs and combined them, or cut the cluster or the watermelon, he is obligated to tithe, for behold he acquired only what was detached. But if he said to him, "Here is an issar for these twenty figs, for these two clusters, for these two pomegranates, for these two watermelons"—he cuts in his usual way and eats casually and is exempt, for they were not fixed by sale, for behold he took them attached:',
  '98#main':
    'One who exchanges with his fellow—this one to eat and that one to eat—both are fixed for ma\'asrot, for behold they took detached. This one to cut and that one to cut—both are not fixed, for sale does not fix a thing whose work was not completed. One took the fruits he exchanged to eat, and one took his exchange to cut them—this one who took to eat is obligated to tithe, and this one who took to cut is not fixed for ma\'aser:',
  '99#main':
    'An am ha\'aretz who was passing in the market and said, "Take for yourselves figs"—they eat and are exempt, for a gift does not fix:',
  '100#main':
    'One who hires workers to work with him in fruits—whether detached or attached—since they have permission d\'oraisa to eat from what they do, behold these eat and are exempt from ma\'aser. And if he stipulated with them that they eat what the Torah did not grant them—such as that the worker stipulated that they eat with his children, or that his son eat in his wages, or that he eat after completing his work from detached fruits—behold this is forbidden to eat until he tithes, since he eats because of the stipulation; behold this is like a buyer:',
  '101#main':
    'One who sends out workers to do work for him in the field—while they have no sustenance from him, they eat from fruits in the field and are exempt from ma\'aser, provided their work is not completed. But if they have sustenance from him, they may not eat, even though their work is not completed, for one does not pay a debt from tevel; [but] they eat one by one from the fig tree, but not from the basket, nor from the box, nor from what was set aside:',
  '102#main':
    'Whether one cooks, or boils, or pickles—he fixes for ma\'aser. But one who smokes fruits until he makes them fit—behold this is a safek:',
  '103#main':
    'One who buries fruits in earth, or in manure, or in straw until he makes them fit to eat—they are not fixed for ma\'aser:',
  '104#main':
    'One who puts wine into a hot dish, or puts oil into a pot in a cauldron when they are boiling—they are fixed for ma\'aser:',
  '105#main':
    'One who mixes wine in hot water—it is fixed; and needless to say if he cooked the wine; and even in the winepress it is forbidden to drink from it until he tithes:',
  '106#main':
    'One who squeezes a cluster into a cup—it is fixed; into a bowl—it is not fixed:',
  '107#main':
    'One who salts fruits in the field—they are fixed. Tevel olives—one by one in salt and he eats—he is exempt:',
  '108#main':
    'One who splits olives so that the oil comes out of them—is exempt:',
  '109#main':
    'One who takes olives from the press—he dips one by one in salt and eats; and if he salted and placed before him, he is obligated. And likewise everything similar:',
  '110#main':
    'One who separates terumah from his fruits—terumah that requires separating a second terumah after it—is fixed for ma\'aser, and he may not eat from them casually until he takes out the second terumah and tithes:',
  '111#main':
    'Fruits whose work was completed and night of Shabbat fell upon them—they are fixed, and he may not eat from them even after Shabbat until he tithes:',
  '112#main':
    'Children who buried figs for Shabbat and forgot to tithe them—they may not eat after Shabbat until they tithe:',
  '113#main':
    'A fig tree that was designated for him to eat its fruits on Shabbat and he gathered a basket from it—he may not eat until he tithes, since these fruits are designated for Shabbat and Shabbat fixes:',
  '114#main':
    'He was eating from a cluster and night of Shabbat fell upon him—he may not complete his eating on Shabbat until he tithes. And if he left them after Shabbat—behold he completes them:',
  '115#main':
    'Everything forbidden to zarim to eat in terumah—such as pits and the like—so too it is forbidden to eat it from tevel and ma\'aser from which terumah was not taken, and ma\'aser sheni, and hekdesh that was not redeemed. And everything permitted to zarim to eat of these things in terumah—so too it is permitted in tevel and ma\'aser from which terumah was not taken, and ma\'aser sheni, and hekdesh that was not redeemed:',
  '116#main':
    'One does not kindle with tevel that is tamei; one does not cover with tevel; one does not sow tevel. And even fruits whose work was not completed—it is forbidden to sow from them until he tithes. In what case? In grain and legumes and the like; but one who uproots saplings that have fruits from place to place within his field—behold this is permitted and it is not like sowing tevel, for behold he did not gather the fruits. And likewise one who uproots turnips and radishes and plants them elsewhere—if he intended to add to their body, it is permitted; and if he planted them so they harden and he takes their seed, it is forbidden, because it is like sowing wheat or barley of tevel:',
  '117#main':
    'One does not sell tevel except for need, and to a chaver; and it is forbidden to send tevel, and even chaver to chaver, lest they rely on each other and eat tevel:',
  '118#main':
    'One who sells fruits to his fellow—the seller says, "On condition that they are tevel I sold them," and the buyer says, "I did not buy from you except tithed"—they compel the seller to fix them; it is a fine for him because he sold tevel:',
  '119#main':
    'One does not pay a debt from tevel, because it is like his sale:',
  '120#main':
    'One who buys tevel from two places—he tithes from this for that:',
  '121#main':
    'One who leases a field from a non-Jew—he tithes and gives to him. One who receives his ancestral field from a non-Jew—he tithes and gives to him:',
  '122#main':
    'A kohen or Levi who bought fruits from an Israelite after their work was completed—they take out terumah and ma\'asrot from their hands and give them to other kohanim and Levites:',
  '123#main':
    'A kohen or Levi who sold detached fruits before their work was completed—and needless to say if he sold while attached—behold the terumah and ma\'aser are theirs:',
  '124#main':
    'One who gives his field in tenancy to a non-Jew or to one who is not trusted regarding ma\'asrot—even though they did not reach the season of ma\'asrot, he must tithe on their account. He gave it to an am ha\'aretz before they reached the season of ma\'asrot—he need not tithe on their account; and once they reach the season of ma\'asrot, he must tithe on their account. How does he do it? He stands on the threshing floor and takes, and he is not concerned for what they ate, for we are not responsible for them:',
  '125#main':
    'On the 1st of Tishrei is Rosh Hashanah for ma\'aser of grain, legumes, and vegetables; and on the 15th of Shevat is Rosh Hashanah for ma\'aser of trees. How so? Grain and legumes that reached the season of ma\'asrot before Rosh Hashanah of the third year—even though they were completed and gathered in the third year, one separates from them ma\'aser sheni. And likewise fruits of the tree that reached the season of ma\'asrot before the 15th of Shevat of the third year—even though they were completed and gathered afterward at the end of the third year, they are tithed retroactively and one separates from them ma\'aser sheni. And likewise if they reached the season of ma\'asrot before the 15th of Shevat of the fourth year—even though they were completed and gathered in the fourth year, one separates from them ma\'aser ani. And if they reached the season of ma\'asrot after the 15th of Shevat, they are tithed prospectively:',
  '126#main':
    'A vegetable—at the time of its harvest is its tithing. How so? If it was harvested on Rosh Hashanah of the third year—even though it reached the season of ma\'asrot and was completed in the second year, one separates from it ma\'aser ani; and if it was harvested in the fourth year, ma\'aser sheni. And likewise the etrog alone from the rest of the fruits of the tree—behold it is like a vegetable and we follow its harvest, both for ma\'aser and for shevi\'it. How so? If it was harvested in the third year after the 15th of Shevat, one separates from it ma\'aser ani, even though it was completed in the second year. And likewise if it was harvested in the fourth year before the 15th of Shevat, one separates from it ma\'aser ani. If it was harvested in the fifth year after the 15th of Shevat, one separates from it ma\'aser sheni—even though we follow its harvest. An etrog in its sixth year that entered the shevi\'it—even if it was like an olive and became like a loaf, it is obligated in ma\'asrot:',
  '127#main':
    'The caperberries of the caper bush—they apply to them the stringencies of the tree and the stringencies of seeds: if they were in the second year that entered the third and were harvested before the 15th of Shevat, one separates ma\'aser rishon and afterward separates another ma\'aser and redeems it, and after he redeems it he gives it to the poor:',
  '128#main':
    'Fruits of the second year that became mixed with fruits of the third, or of the third with the fourth—we follow the majority. Half and half—one separates ma\'aser sheni from everything, but not ma\'aser ani, for ma\'aser sheni is stringent since behold it is kodesh, and ma\'aser ani is chullin. And likewise fruits that are in doubt whether they are second-year fruits or third-year fruits—one separates from them ma\'aser sheni:',
  '129#main':
    'Everything exempt from ma\'aser rishon is exempt from ma\'aser sheni and from ma\'aser ani; and everything obligated in rishon is obligated in both of them. And everything that separates ma\'aser takes out ma\'aser; and everything that does not separate ma\'aser does not take out ma\'aser. And everyone for whom if he separated terumah his terumah is terumah—so too if he tithed, behold it is ma\'aser. And everyone whose terumah is not terumah—so too if he took out ma\'aser, it is not ma\'aser:',
  '130#main':
    'Fruits from which he took out ma\'aser rishon before they were fixed for ma\'aser—he may eat from them casually before he takes out ma\'aser sheni, for rishon does not fix sheni. But once they were fixed for ma\'aser, even though he took out rishon, it is forbidden to eat from them casually until he takes out sheni or ma\'aser ani:',
  '131#main':
    'They did not obligate separating ma\'aser sheni in Syria:',
  '132#main':
    'Ma\'aser sheni is eaten by its owner inside the wall of Jerusalem, as it is said, "And you shall eat before the Lord your God in the place that He will choose," etc. And it applies whether when the Temple stands or when it does not stand; but it is not eaten in Jerusalem except when the Temple stands, as it is said, "The tithe of your grain, your wine, your oil, and the firstlings of your cattle and flocks"—from tradition they learned: just as a bechor is eaten only before the Temple, so too ma\'aser sheni is eaten only before the Temple:',
  '133#main':
    'At this time, if he wished to redeem ma\'aser sheni worth a maneh for a perutah l\'chatchila, he redeems it and throws the perutah into the Great Sea; but for other rivers he must invalidate it first. And likewise if he profaned (meaning: if he made it chullin through redemption) ma\'aser worth a maneh on worth of a perutah from other fruits—behold it is profaned, and he burns the fruits on which he profaned so that there not be a stumbling block for others:',
  '134#main':
    'And the measure of a perutah is like half a barley grain of refined silver, which is a part of 1/32 of a silver ma\'ah, which is an Ashpira Ottomani whose weight is a quarter dirham; thus the perutah is a part of 1/48 of a kate\'a and a little more, and one must be precise that the fruits on which he profanes ma\'aser sheni are worth at least so much. (And see above siman 294 seif 6.)',
  '135#main':
    'Just as we do not eat ma\'aser sheni at this time in Jerusalem, so we do not redeem it there, nor profane it, nor sell it. And if it entered Jerusalem—even at this time—we do not take it out from there, and we leave it there until it rots. [And likewise if he transgressed and took it out from there, we leave it until it rots.] Therefore one does not separate ma\'aser sheni in Jerusalem at this time, but takes the fruits out tevel outside the city and separates it there and redeems it. And if he separated them there at this time, they rot. In what case? In ma\'aser sheni that did not become tamei; but if it became tamei, one redeems it in Jerusalem. (And one should make it fit so that it receives tumah and he will have permission in redemption) (Tur):',
  '136#main':
    'One who redeems ma\'aser sheni says, "These fruits are profaned on these coins." And if he did not specify but separated coins corresponding to the fruits, it is enough, and he need not specify, and the fruits emerge to chullin:',
  '137#main':
    'One who redeems ma\'aser sheni blesses "...Who commanded us regarding the redemption of ma\'aser sheni." And if he profaned them on other fruits, he blesses on the profanation of ma\'aser sheni. And one who redeems or profanes ma\'aser sheni of demai does not require a blessing:',
  '138#main':
    'One redeems ma\'aser sheni only with money; and likewise if he redeemed his ma\'aser sheni that he adds a fifth—the fifth shall be only money like the principal. And he does not redeem with money that is not coin but with minted money that has a form or writing on it. And if he redeemed with the language of money and the like, which is called asimon, he has done nothing. And one does not redeem with less than a perutah, because it is like redeeming with asimon. And one does not redeem with a coin that does not circulate at that time and in that place. And he does not redeem with money that is not in his possession. And it is possible that these matters are said only when the Temple stood; but now, when ma\'aser sheni is not eaten, one need not be strict about this; nevertheless one should be concerned about the matter, and even at this time one should not redeem ma\'aser sheni except on money that has circulating coinage and is in his possession, or profane it on other fruits:',
  '139#main':
    'One who redeems ma\'aser sheni before separating it—such as if he said, "The ma\'aser sheni of these fruits is redeemed on these coins"—he has said nothing and did not fix ma\'aser. But if he fixed it and said, "The ma\'aser that is in the north or in the south is profaned on these coins"—behold it is redeemed:',
  '140#main':
    'It is a positive mitzvah to confess before the Lord after one takes out all the gifts of the seed of the land, and this is called viduy ma\'aser. And one confesses this confession only after the year in which one separates ma\'aser ani:',
  '141#main':
    'When does one confess? At minchah on the last Yom Tov of Pesach of the fourth and seventh years:',
  '142#main':
    'One confesses only by day, and the whole day is fit for viduy ma\'aser, whether when the Temple stands or when it does not stand. One is obligated to destroy and to confess:',
  '143#main':
    'And this confession is said in every language; and if many wished to confess as one, they confess:',
  '144#main':
    'One does not confess until one of the gifts remains with him. And on the eve of the last Yom Tov was the destruction, and on the morrow one confesses. And fruits that did not reach the season of ma\'asrot at the time of destruction do not hold him back from confessing, and he is not obligated to destroy them:',
  '145#main':
    'One whose fruits were far from him and the day of destruction arrived—behold he calls the name of the gifts and transfers them to their owners on the land, or to one who transfers them to their owners, and confesses on the morrow:',
  '146#main':
    'If he separated the gifts not in order, or if his tevel was burned, or if he separated ma\'aser in tumah—he does not confess: {Rama: This author omitted all the laws of ma\'aser ani that the Tur wrote, from which one learns many laws of tzedakah practiced nowadays; and it is possible he relied on what was explained in the laws of tzedakah. But also in the laws of terumot and ma\'asrot he omitted several laws that the Tur wrote and inserted others in their place; and all this caused him to copy the words of the Rambam, Laws of Terumot and Ma\'asrot, as written and in their language, and he did not pay attention to other matters. And one who wishes to stand on the principles of the laws should look inside.}',
};
